WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
SPEAKER: Today I'm
going to show you

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.040
how to create a table
in BigQuery with Ruby.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.700
I'm creating a table to
track my cat pictures.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.659
The Google Cloud BigQuery gem
is the easiest way to do this.

00:00:08.659 --> 00:00:10.950
To use the gem you need a
project and a service account

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:11.440
key.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.440
I created these earlier.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.740
Now to create my table.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.295
First, I require Google Cloud.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.950
Now I'm going to create
a BigQuery object.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.100
Groups of tables in BigQuery
are called data sets,

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.370
so I'm creating a data
set called Pictures.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.710
Now I can create the table.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.410
I'm naming it Cats,
and I'm passing

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:39.740
in the schema as a block.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.230
This is very similar to
creating migrations in Rails,

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:43.412
so it should feel natural.

00:00:50.071 --> 00:00:50.570
That's it.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.150
Now I have a table in BigQuery.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.617
To verify that, you can go to
the BigQuery console, reload,

00:00:56.617 --> 00:00:58.200
and you'll see there
is my cats table.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.640
I hope you found this helpful.

